# They Asked for Life; What They Received Was Death!
Tamil title: கேட்டது வாழ்வு! கிடைத்ததூ சாவு!

All thinking people in the country have been grieved by the news that Communist prisoners were shot dead in Salem prison.

Twenty-two people became corpses; more than a hundred were wounded. This news is capable of creating an uproar not merely in our country, but throughout the world.

Not even one month has passed since the republic dawned, and already the tyranny of the gun is at work inside a prison. Democracy has blossomed, while the stench of corpses spreads.

“Rama Rajya” is in operation, and there are corpses in the prison!

This terrible event, which makes the heart tremble merely on hearing of it, has occurred under the rule of those who called themselves partners of the poor.

They showed the spinning wheel to the people before; now the gun speaks.

It is said that the Communist prisoners used whatever came to hand to attack the prison officers, and that twenty-two were therefore shot dead while more than 137 lay wounded.

The authorities say this was the reason gunfire had to be used, and the government too has issued a statement.

Bullets played upon 220 prisoners confined in a small space within a jail protected on all four sides by fences and walls. Twenty-two lives were sent to the world of death. One hundred and thirty-seven bodies, struck by bullets, now lie in hospital receiving treatment, the government announces!

This sorrowful tragedy, which shakes human hearts, has turned a page of our country's history bloody.

The country believed that those who had endured suffering and acquired the scars of sacrifice would bring about good. The people expected that a popular government would arise, that there would be no court of repression, and that distress would end. They expected a great deal. But hope is dissolving and grief is spreading!

The support of people distressed by the cruelty of white imperialism and foreign rule built the Congress Party, in the hope that benefits would bloom when “our government” dawned. That those who ascended the seat of power should have forgotten this truth so quickly is not merely ironic; it is pitiable!

Twenty-two lives: the heart boils merely to think of them. They have become corpses and can no longer be seen on the stage of this world! Yes, they died. No, they were put to death!

Neither the country nor the world could have expected such events under the rule of those who went about speaking of nonviolence and love. Yet today such incidents have become daily occurrences!

Every day brings reports of gunfire upon students in Bareilly, Nasik and Calcutta. Near Palani, a Communist is said to have fled when officers went to arrest him. The gun was immediately put to work. Four days ago he fell to the ground as a corpse.

Those who said they would govern by trusting in love now conduct tyranny at the point of a gun. The same scene is unfolding in every province of India: government by the gun.

We have never liked violence or sabotage, not merely today but at any time. We believe that they can only ruin a country and lay waste a fine flower garden; good cannot blossom through them. When Congress friends who now occupy the seat of power engaged in destructive acts that disordered the country during the “August” disturbances, calling them revolution, we condemned them then as well. We said that this was not the path to rights. We condemn such action today too. Whether it is the Communist Party, the Socialists or any other party, we have consistently said that calling violence to one's aid is improper and must not be done.

Whoever plunders the people's lives by engaging in sabotage must be restrained. We do not deny that.

But no person with a human heart can tolerate accusing an entire party of “sabotage,” seizing and imprisoning everyone who can be caught, and shooting down anyone who runs before being captured.

The course of the Communist Party is disliked. On the claim that it engages in sabotage, the party has been made illegal in some provinces.

In other provinces it has not been made illegal.

Because the authorities dislike its course, however, the government has used emergency laws to confine thousands in prisons, not for months but for years!

Some of today's rulers were themselves detained in prison as security prisoners under British rule. The demonstrations and meetings of condemnation held outside at the time have not yet faded from memory. “Imprisoning people without trial! Are they human beings or beasts? Down with white imperialism!” Such heroic cries were raised not only by Congress members but by the general public. The country cannot have forgotten the many cries of condemnation raised by those outside and by the “national newspapers” whenever even a minor incident occurred in a prison. From village to city, public assemblies seethed: “The white man imprisons and tortures those who labour for self-rule. What an outrage!”

“Would this evil occur if we had our own government?” today's Congress rulers asked the people then. “Yes, foreign rule is tyrannical rule! It must end,” the people cried.

They began governing the country under the sacred name of “popular government.” The people rejoiced that “our government” had arrived. Yet there has been no change in its course, rule or excesses!

A party or individual engaged in sabotage must indeed be restrained. No well-wisher will disagree that troublemakers who inflict suffering upon the people must be controlled.

But civilised people cannot accept invoking “sabotage” as a pretext, seizing everyone in sight, throwing them into prison and committing excesses.

There are courts to try and punish those who commit crimes. There are judges to hear cases against those involved in sabotage and deliver verdicts. That the friends of “Rama Rajya” dislike this honest path is not a surprising incident; it is one filled with pain!

It is intolerable to arrest everyone under suspicion through emergency law, throw them into prison, and hold them for years without trial.

Today's rulers now embrace the very methods that they once denounced in anguish as intolerable cruelty. They proceed along the path of repression! The tyranny of the gun persists because of the arrogance and indifference born of thinking, “After all, we are the ones in power.”

The anguish and agitation among security prisoners locked away without trial did not begin today or yesterday; it has arisen and grown over many months. With the slogan “Trial or release,” they have repeatedly embarked upon various forms of protest. Though the rulers know all this, they continue to regard repression as their proper course!

The result? Alas, what misery! Twenty-two lives became corpses!

They were shot dead!

We learn that the government has ordered an inquiry into this incident, so full of misery and cruelty. It is essential that the inquiry panel include someone representing the public. A former judge, a lawyer and a collector alone are not enough. There must also be a person with public ties who can echo the people's thoughts. There are labour leaders such as Thiru. Vi. Ka. and Sarkarai!

It will not suffice merely to stage something under the name of an inquiry. Those occupying the seat of power today must answer before the people's assembly tomorrow; ministers are not immortal!

A popular government means one that maintains the country in good order without disturbance and destruction. The people call only a government that makes happiness bloom among them a people's government.

Those with understanding call it true government only when it meets harm and injury to the people honestly, with justice as its aid.

If it forgets justice, seizes and imprisons whomever it pleases, and inflicts suffering, it can only be called a government of excess! People broken by the outrages of foreign rule ask for life and demand well-being, not bullets! They ask for life, not death! In pain, the people's hearts lie boiling. Their eyes brim with tears as they stand immersed in sorrow, remembering the corpses and counting the bullets.

We wish to show that sight to the rulers.

The people's assembly is a swelling sea, an erupting mountain. Do not play with its anguish. We wish to echo the people's cry: “Give us life; we do not want death!”

(*Dravida Nadu*, 19 February 1950)

---NOTES---
The article concerns the police firing on Communist detainees in Salem Central Prison in February 1950. `பாதுகாப்புக் கைதிகள்` is rendered “security prisoners,” meaning preventive detainees held without ordinary trial. `திரு.வி.க.` refers to Tamil scholar and labour leader Thiru. V. Kalyanasundaram; `சக்கரை` refers to labour leader V. Chakkarai Chettiar. The source reports both “more than a hundred” wounded and a later figure of more than 137; both formulations are retained. The title contains the OCR form `கிடைத்ததூ`, preserved verbatim in the Tamil title override rather than silently normalised.
